+\medskip
+\noindent{\bf Claim 2 (a derivation after a complete development).}
+Let $\redexset$ be a set of coinitial steps and $\redseqthree$ a complete development of $\redexset$.
+Then for every derivation $\redseq$ coinitial with $\redseqthree$:
+\[
+  \names((\redseq/\redseqthree)/(\tm'/\redseqthree)) = \names(\redseq/\tm') \setminus \names(\redexset/\tm')
+\]
+By induction on $\redseq$, generalizing over $\tm$, $\tm'$, $\redexset$ and $\redseqthree$.
+The empty case is immediate. Let $\redseq = \redex\redseq'$.
+Then $\redseq/\redseqthree = (\redex/\redseqthree)\,(\redseq'/(\redseqthree/\redex))$,
+where $\redex/\redseqthree$ stands for a complete development of the residual set,
+and $\redseqthree/\redex$ is a complete development of $\redexset/\redex$.
+The names of $(\redseq/\redseqthree)/(\tm'/\redseqthree)$ are the union of two parts:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item $\names((\redex/\redseqthree)/(\tm'/\redseqthree)) = \names(\redex/\tm') \setminus \names(\redexset/\tm')$,
+  by \rlem{simulation_residual_of_a_development}, Claim~1, and $\names(\redseqthree/\tm') = \names(\redexset/\tm')$.
+\item $\names((\redseq'/(\redseqthree/\redex))/((\tm'/\redseqthree)/(\redex/\redseqthree)))$.
+  Since $\redseqthree(\redex/\redseqthree) \permeq \redex(\redseqthree/\redex)$,
+  item~1 of \rprop{compatibility_of_simulation_residuals_and_permutation_equivalence} gives
+  $(\tm'/\redseqthree)/(\redex/\redseqthree) = (\tm'/\redex)/(\redseqthree/\redex)$.
+  So by \ih{} (on $\tm'/\redex$, the set $\redexset/\redex$, and its development $\redseqthree/\redex$),
+  this part is $\names(\redseq'/(\tm'/\redex)) \setminus \names((\redexset/\redex)/(\tm'/\redex))$.
+  By \rlem{cube_lemma_for_simulation_residuals} applied to each step of $\redexset$,
+  $\names((\redexset/\redex)/(\tm'/\redex)) = \names(\redexset/\tm') \setminus \names(\redex/\tm')$.
+\end{itemize}
+By \rlem{names_concatenation_disjoint_union} applied to $\redseq/\tm' = (\redex/\tm')(\redseq'/(\tm'/\redex))$,
+the sets $\names(\redex/\tm')$ and $\names(\redseq'/(\tm'/\redex))$ are disjoint,
+so the second part equals $\names(\redseq'/(\tm'/\redex)) \setminus \names(\redexset/\tm')$.
+The union of both parts is
+$(\names(\redex/\tm') \cup \names(\redseq'/(\tm'/\redex))) \setminus \names(\redexset/\tm')
+ = \names(\redseq/\tm') \setminus \names(\redexset/\tm')$.
+
+\medskip
+\noindent{\bf Claim 3 (the equation \eqref{eq:cubelemma_names}).}
+By induction on $\redseqtwo$. The empty case is immediate.
+If $\redseqtwo = \redextwo\redseqtwo'$, then $\redseq/\redseqtwo = (\redseq/\redextwo)/\redseqtwo'$
+and $\tm'/\redseqtwo = (\tm'/\redextwo)/\redseqtwo'$, so:
+\[
+  \begin{array}{rcll}
+  \names((\redseq/\redseqtwo)/(\tm'/\redseqtwo))
+    & = & \names((\redseq/\redextwo)/(\tm'/\redextwo)) \setminus \names(\redseqtwo'/(\tm'/\redextwo)) & \text{by \ih} \\
+    & = & (\names(\redseq/\tm') \setminus \names(\redextwo/\tm')) \setminus \names(\redseqtwo'/(\tm'/\redextwo)) & \text{by Claim~2 with $\redexset = \set{\redextwo}$} \\
+    & = & \names(\redseq/\tm') \setminus \names(\redseqtwo/\tm') & \text{since $\redseqtwo/\tm' = (\redextwo/\tm')(\redseqtwo'/(\tm'/\redextwo))$}
+  \end{array}
+\]
 \end{proof}
 
 
